    description: "Apply one structural edit to the connected viewsheet. Ops: " + EDIT_OPS.join(", ") + ".\n\n`add` is also how a new chart, crosstab, table, or freehand table gets placed on the canvas \u2014 the same backend call the Composer's own drag-and-drop from the Insert toolbox uses. See the `type` field for the full list.\n\nEach call is exactly one undo checkpoint, so the user's Ctrl+Z steps back through your edits one at a time.\n\nA clean return means the edit was accepted, not that the result looks right \u2014 call get_viewsheet_image afterwards to actually see it.\n\nThis tool cannot change what data an assembly shows. For that use the binding tools \u2014 set_chart_shelf, set_chart_single_shelf, set_aesthetic_field \u2014 on the same session.\n\n`group` combines two or more assemblies into a GroupContainer (a plain layout grouping \u2014 moving/resizing it moves/resizes its contents together). `group_as_tab` combines them into a Tab instead (a tabbed view where only one child is visible at a time, switched by clicking its tab strip) \u2014 the first two `assemblies` form the tab and each further one is added to that same tab. `group_as_selection_container` combines two or more Selection List/Selection Tree/Time Slider/Calendar filters into one compacted Selection Container panel (the same container the Composer's own \"drop a filter onto an existing Selection Container\" gesture builds) \u2014 every named assembly must already be one of those filter types, or the call fails naming the offending one. `ungroup` reverses a GroupContainer created by `group`; it does NOT reverse a Tab created by `group_as_tab` or a Selection Container created by `group_as_selection_container` \u2014 to take an assembly back out of either, use `move_from_container` on it instead. A Tab is removed automatically once only one assembly remains in it; a Selection Container is NOT \u2014 remove it explicitly with `edit(op:'remove')` once empty.\n\n`refresh` forces one assembly to re-execute its query and re-render \u2014 use it after a worksheet-side change (e.g. a ranking/aggregate edit) made after the chart was already bound; refresh_data (on the worksheet session) does not reach bound viewsheets.\n\n`refresh_viewsheet` forces the WHOLE sheet to reset and re-render \u2014 no `assembly` needed. It is the same sheet-level refresh the Composer UI's own Refresh toolbar button performs, and the only recovery path if a prior get_viewsheet_image() call with no `target` left this session unable to render at all: calling this once clears it, same as a human clicking Refresh would.",
